    "tagline": "Verify each unit of logic in isolation, fast and repeatably.",
    "keywords": "Unit tests verify a single class or method in isolation — no database, no HTTP, no file system. The most popular framework in .NET is xUnit , which discovers tests by convention: any public class with a [Fact] (single case) or [Theory] (multiple inputs via [InlineData] ) method is a test. Assertions use Assert.Equal , Assert.True , Assert.Throws , etc. Isolating dependencies is done with mocks — test doubles that fake external behaviour. The Moq library is the standard choice: new Mock&lt;IMyService&gt;() creates a mock, .Setup(...).Returns(...) configures it, and .Verify(...) asserts it was called. The Arrange-Act-Assert pattern keeps tests readable: set up, run, check. [Fact] marks a single test; [Theory] with [InlineData] runs it with multiple inputs Mock dependencies with Moq so tests "
