    "tagline": "Measure first, then apply the right tool — most performance gains come from a handful of patterns.",
    "keywords": "Performance in .NET nearly always starts with measurement : BenchmarkDotNet for micro-benchmarks, dotnet-trace or Visual Studio's profiler for application-level profiling. Guessing without data wastes effort and risks making things worse. The highest-leverage patterns: avoid allocations in hot paths (use Span&lt;T&gt; , ArrayPool&lt;T&gt; , StringBuilder ); prefer async/await for I/O so threads aren't blocked; use Parallel.ForEach or PLINQ only for CPU-bound work; avoid async void , unnecessary Task.Result , and Thread.Sleep ; and keep LINQ chains over large sets out of hot paths — they allocate enumerators and delegates. ValueTask instead of Task avoids allocations when a result is often available synchronously. Profile before optimising — BenchmarkDotNet and dotnet-trace show where time "
