    "tagline": "Use ?. on the left side of an assignment.",
    "keywords": "Before C# 14, safely assigning to a property on a possibly-null object required an explicit if check first. Null-conditional assignment lets you write customer?.Name = \"Guest\" directly — the right-hand side is only evaluated and assigned when the receiver isn't null, and the whole statement is a no-op otherwise. It also works with compound assignment operators like += and -= , though not with ++ or -- . Works with compound assignment too, e.g. += and -= Not supported for ++ or -- The right-hand expression isn’t evaluated at all when the receiver is null Using ??= when you actually want to overwrite existing values — ??= only assigns if the left side is null Forgetting that ??= is not atomic — it's not thread-safe without additional synchronisation Chaining ??= with side-effectful right-han"
