    "keywords": "The Adapter pattern wraps an existing class behind a new interface so it can be used where that interface is expected, without modifying the original class. It's the classic fix for plugging a third-party or legacy API into code written against your own abstraction. Unlike Decorator, which adds behavior while keeping the same interface, Adapter's job is purely translation — converting one shape of API into another. Adapter translates one interface into another without changing the original Common when integrating a third-party or legacy class into your own abstraction Differs from Decorator, which adds behavior rather than changing the interface Creating an adapter when you could change the original class — adapters add indirection; prefer direct changes when possible Making the adapter do"
