    "keywords": "The Command pattern wraps an action and its parameters inside an object implementing a common interface (typically a single Execute() method), decoupling the code that invokes an action from the code that knows how to perform it. Because the request is now an object, it can be queued, logged, retried, or paired with an Undo() method — this is the pattern behind menu actions, task queues, and undo/redo stacks in editors. Command wraps a request as an object with a common Execute() interface Decouples the invoker (button, queue) from the receiver that does the work Naturally supports queuing, logging, retrying, and undo/redo Commands that directly modify shared state without going through a queue or handler — loses the undo/replay benefit Making commands too coarse — one command per user act"
