    "keywords": "A JSON Web Token (JWT) is a compact, URL-safe string carrying claims — assertions about a user, like their id or roles — that are cryptographically signed so a server can verify them without hitting a database. It has three base64-encoded parts separated by dots: header (algorithm), payload (claims), and signature . In ASP.NET Core, the AddJwtBearer extension registers middleware that reads the Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt; header, validates the signature and expiry against your configured options, and populates HttpContext.User with the token's claims — making them available to authorization policies and your own code. JWTs are signed, not encrypted — never put secrets in the payload Validate issuer, audience, and expiry — all three matter for real security Short expiry (15 min) + r"
