    "tagline": "CORS lets browsers call your API from a different origin — you control which ones.",
    "keywords": "The browser's same-origin policy blocks JavaScript from making requests to a different origin (scheme + host + port). CORS is the W3C mechanism that lets a server explicitly permit cross-origin requests by including specific response headers. Without CORS, your React/Vue/Angular frontend hosted on app.example.com cannot call your API on api.example.com . Before state-changing requests (POST, PUT, DELETE) the browser sends a preflight OPTIONS request. Your API must respond with the correct Access-Control-Allow-* headers or the browser blocks the actual request. ASP.NET Core's CORS middleware handles all of this automatically once you configure policies. UseCors() must be placed after UseRouting() and before UseAuthorization() Named policies let you apply different CORS rules to different en"
